fact WorkItem(id: "WI-765", description: some(value: "Reject a DUPLICATE-KEY named tuple TYPE language-wide. Today `operation f() -> (a: Int64, a: String)` LOADS CLEAN -- `convert_tuple_type` pushes both components into its `named` vec and `make_named_tuple_type` builds two fields with the same symbol, so the type carries two columns named `a` that no field lookup (WI-638 mode 3, dot dispatch, `projection_columns`) can answer unambiguously. MEASURED, not assumed: probed during WI-763 and it produced zero diagnostics. WI-763 closed the ONE surface it made newly reachable -- a written `Project` keep spec `Keep = (a: \"name\", a: \"age\")`, now loud in `keep_spec_projections` (typing.rs, search 'names the result key') -- but that is a keep-spec-local guard, not the general rule. The general rule belongs at the PRODUCER: `convert_tuple_type` (parse/convert.rs), where the all-or-nothing positional/named check already lives, so every consumer of a named-tuple type inherits it. NOTE the positional path desugars to `_1.._N` and cannot collide, so only the explicitly-named path needs the check. SCOPE RISK to measure FIRST: a duplicate-key tuple may already exist in stdlib/ or examples/ and would start failing -- run the full workspace before assuming this is additive. Consider whether the same check is owed to entity constructor field lists (`entity e(a: Int64, a: String)`) and arrow param lists, which are separate walkers. ACCEPTANCE: a duplicate-key named tuple type is a loud located error naming the repeated key; keep-spec duplicates keep their more specific message; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-763"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-22T09:01:14Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-765", author: "user", content: "Already delivered by WI-805, which put the rule at exactly the producer this ticket named (convert_tuple_type -> check_label_unique, parse/convert.rs). Verified by driving the ticket's own headline case: 'operation take(t: (a: Int64, a: String)) -> String' is refused at PARSE with 'duplicate tuple type component label `a`', located at the second `a`. The two 'consider whether' extensions are also settled: entity field lists covered by WI-808, named-arg lists by WI-809, arrow parameter lists deliberately EXCLUDED with a measured rationale (params apply positionally, so a repeated binder leaves nothing unreachable and its type is still checked at every call). Keep-spec duplicates keep their more specific message (WI-763). SCOPE RISK the ticket flagged did not materialize: no duplicate-key tuple existed in stdlib/ or examples/ -- full workspace green, 3395 tests. GAP CLOSED HERE: acceptance said 'loud LOCATED error', but only the LITERAL side was span-pinned; the type side asserted the message alone, which a diagnostic reported at the tuple's '(' or at the FIRST label would satisfy just as well. Added a span-pinned test and verified it is load-bearing by a positive control -- reporting at the component instead of its name node makes it fail (99..108 vs 99..100).", at: "2026-07-22T09:01:28Z")
